A||||27||shain||honest opinion||||9||
Z||000000||shain||07-06-2000||07:24 AM||||ok, let get one thing straight! i have wanted to own a fiero since i was a litle kid. now i have a 2m4(aka the peice of crap). lets face it it sucks. now, a lot of people i have talked to have told me not to buya v6 fiero. they said it gives nothing but headaches. i dont want to believe this. i love the styling of the late gt's and i want one...bad!! but is this an ideal car for me to own keeping in mind that i also have to go to college, and am not going to have time/money, to do extensive repairs. <BR>please let me know your honest opinions, i have my heart set on a fiero, but you guys know best i guess.<P>shain<BR>---------<BR>crappy 85 2m4<BR>nothing done to it <BR>everything going wrong!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000001||contra||07-06-2000||07:38 AM||||I dont know much about the engine work in the 4cyl fieros because i've never owned one, but i currently own 3 V6 fieros and they've all had or have engine problems but nothing that is hard to fix or will bust my wallet. I think the only large problem on my GT is the cracked manifold. I have a replacement manifold, i just have to figure out how to switch them  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>My honest opinion is though, if you want a GT get either a 1986 GT or 1987 GT. The 88's look like they cost a little more on repairs sometimes. Also, i would spend that little extra cash on a fiero with the lowest milage you can find, and has hardly any cosmetic problems. That way you wont have to work on making it look nice, you can just save your time and just drive it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||07-06-2000||07:41 AM||||I have an 85SE v-6 4spd.  Other than replacing the clutch I've had no more problems with this car than any of my other cars with similar age and mileage (128000 miles).<P>I don't believe the v-6 is any more trouble than the 4 cyl. other than having to spend another 15 minutes to replace the front spark plugs.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||lennyb4||07-06-2000||07:42 AM||||Shane, I have to ask why you say your iron duck is crap? I have an 87 Sport Coupe w/the duck, 95,000 miles. I had to do a complete tune up including coils & module and now it runs like a charm. The duck may have its problems now and them but anyone will tell you that once you get the bugs worked out it will run for-ever. As for the V-6, i have a friend with a 87 GT and he really doesnt have alot of problems with it. Parts are more expensive then the ducks. BUT you have to remember that these cars are 12-16 years old and there's gonna be problems. But if you stick it out you will have a car to be proud of (not to mention thier getting harder to find everyday).||||reg||6
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||07-06-2000||07:46 AM||||   I don't believe that the 2M4 was very refined till after 85. I have an 85GT V6 and it was nothing but a headache untill I got involved here, on the forum. There are alot of myths and stigmas about the Fiero, none of which are unbeatable if you excercise good judgement and maintainence practices. These cars are getting old now and you have to keep your eye on them. But that's no different than any other vehical. List your concerns and problems and the forum will be there to assist. It might take a minute to get the car where it should be, but once you get it there all you have to do is take care of it. Buy the V6, Pi$$ on the neysayers.<P>   Late, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||12
Z||000005||shain||07-06-2000||07:56 AM||||why my iron duke pisses me off!!<BR>in the past 2 weeks!! <BR>1. wierd knock from engine<BR>2. stalling when left to idle<BR>3. cooked alt<BR>4.cooked ignition coil and or module<BR>5.new efi setup<BR>6.timing and water pump<BR>7.egr valve<BR>also<BR>no balls!! (21.7 sec is just embarrasing)<BR>sounds like a diesel truck!! (and no its not a hole in the exaust!) does that awnser your question? looks like you got a good one and mine is **** !||||reg||1
Z||000006||lennyb4||07-06-2000||08:50 AM||||Relax bud, just asking a question. I would have to agree that it seems like you have some major engine issues. But like contra said, if your going for a V-6 GT get either an 86 or 87. 88's do have the improved handling (and some other improvements)but you will find that parts are alot more expensive then the 86 or 87 models. If your body and interior are in decent shape and you have a garage or somewhere to store it then you might be able to recoupe some of the purchase cost by parting out your old one.<P>......just my .02*||||reg||6
Z||000007||ChadMan||07-06-2000||09:08 AM||||I don't think any self-respecting Duke should do the 1/4 in more than 19 seconds (maybe 20 if it's an auto - I've never driven an auto Duke).<P>I have owned three Fieros.  Each one cost me a lot of money at the outset, then was very reliable.  The V6's are harder to work on, but aren't any more or less reliable than the Duke IMHO.  All Dukes do sound like clattery crap, though - even the good running ones.<P>It sounds like you're on your way to getting your Fiero debugged.  I'd run a compression check on your Duke - if it checks out good, keep fixing these niggling problems and you'll eventually get it right.  If it checks out bad, unload it on some poor sap or start saving for a V6!<P>Keep the faith!||||reg||5
Z||000008||007DOUG||07-06-2000||09:17 AM||||Since you've already dumped some $$$ into fixing your 4 banger, use it as a beater through college.<P>After you graduate, and have a "real" job, then you can buy the fastback of your dreams.<P>After all, even if you buy a fastback now, you'll still have the reputation of owning a fiero (good in my opion). Plus, you'll have additional $$$ in modifying it to keep up with today's faster 0-60 times (if thats what you're seeking). <P>Or, if you just want the looks, buy a fastback clip and install it on your present fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||07-06-2000||09:36 AM||||'85 GT.  Aero body work, but notchback.  Seems to be cheaper than the fastbacks.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000010||BBBAD GT||07-06-2000||09:36 AM||||Want my opinion?  I sold my perfect daily driver (95 Tracer) for a 87 GT.  I've always wanted a Fiero, and a little more excitement on the way to work (1/2 hr drive one way).  The GT has signficantly more miles on it (88k now), so I took the chance.  So far, I've been happy.  Its only been down one time (darn module), and I'm totally satisfied with it.  The AC blows cold in the 90+ heat, and it gets around 25mph (had 30+ before, but oh well).  <P>Sometimes you have to take a chance.......<P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Stock for now<BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT's Web Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Gold-86SE||07-06-2000||10:03 AM||||<BR>This is fairly long, but be patient.<P>Shain, I'll let you in on a little secret, many new members to this forum come here out of frustration with their Fieros, many to the point of selling.  But once they have spent some time here, learn some of the bug-a-boos with the cars, things work out fine.  The hardest thing to do is keep a good attitude.<P>Why we have Fieros:<BR>We buy the cars because we love them.<BR>We drive the cars because we love them.<BR>And we work on the cars because we love them.<P>When I went to pick up my latest Fiero, my brother expressed interest in a couple of the Fieros this guy had available.  My brother said that he would sell his 99 Firebird and have the Fiero for a daily driver and I politely informed him that Fieros will have to worked on frequently and implied that he should keep the Firebird and have the Fiero as a third auto.  Now, not everyone can do this, but even if yours is your only car, you will still have to work on it - it is just that your time will have to be well managed.<P>Here is truth, if you recently purchased your Fiero, then that person sold it because they did not want to perform any maintenance.  Congratulations, you now have a maintenance mobile for a little while, but you will eventually work the maintenance problems out of the car. It just depends on your patience, your wallet, and your mechanical ability.<P>If you are a mechanic, repairs are easy, however if you are a beginner mechanic, you can follow the guys' instructions on this forum and they will tell you all the things to look for and in some cases, exactly, how to fix the problem.<P>You asked for opionions, so here is my bottom line.  I thought I remember reading about a post regarding stereo equipment and your alternator, as hard as it may be to do, stop spending money on bells and whistles and put that money into the required maintenance for the Fiero.  Once, the problems are gone, then go back to your passion.  The real question is what is the priority.<P>Good luck, and keep the Fiero Faith.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fret Naught||07-06-2000||12:43 PM||||What Gold said, couldn't have put it better myself.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Haze_Performance||07-06-2000||01:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by shain:<BR><B>ok, let get one thing straight! i have wanted to own a fiero since i was a litle kid. now i have a 2m4(aka the peice of crap). lets face it it sucks. now, a lot of people i have talked to have told me not to buya v6 fiero. they said it gives nothing but headaches. i dont want to believe this. i love the styling of the late gt's and i want one...bad!! but is this an ideal car for me to own keeping in mind that i also have to go to college, and am not going to have time/money, to do extensive repairs. <BR>please let me know your honest opinions, i have my heart set on a fiero, but you guys know best i guess.<P>shain<BR>---------<BR>crappy 85 2m4<BR>nothing done to it <BR>everything going wrong!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have a 85 2M4. Ive had 0 problems with it. Its the best car ive ever owned.<P><P>------------------<BR>Carl (85 Fiero) My Website's  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/HMP" TARGET=_blank>Haze Mustang Performance</A>   &  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/WS6_Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero</A>||||reg||14
Z||000014||fldevel||07-06-2000||01:36 PM|||| am i the only fiero baby ? i grew up with fieros almost all my life ,my mother and father got a pair of matching 85 GT's in 85 wich both run to this day. i now own 2 myself ..great little car just need someone with some tlc to give them ..remember fieros are like pets,if u dont give em the attention they need they will turn on ya ||||reg||1
Z||000015||frontal lobe||07-06-2000||02:50 PM||||OK, Shain, I am going to give you the truth here, and then brace myself to be pummeled.  Here goes.  If, as you state, you will not have time or money to work on a Fiero,  you honestly are going to be better off getting some ultra-reliable car like a Honda Civic, Toyota Corolla/Geo Prism, etc.  You will not get automotive excitement, but you will get there and over time have less repairs, cost, and down time.  Now some people may say they have had just as much trouble with a Honda or Civic of similar age and miles, and some have.  But MOST of the time, the Honda's and Toyotas are more reliable.  Let's be honest.<BR>So if I was in your college situation, I'd be getting something like that.<P>I am happy to say I am NOT in college anymore and can afford a 3rd car, which is an '87 Fiero GT 5-speed with 64,000 miles.  It is absolutely a BLAST to drive.  VERY snappy.  Plus it has the advantage of my wife saying she can hear me coming home when I'm about 1/2 mile out!<P>Once you get out of college, I'll be calling, "Come back, Shain; come back, Shain."  Come back to Fiero's.  If you are just going into college, you won't know what I just meant, so ask your grandparents!!||||reg||1
Z||000016||Haze_Performance||07-06-2000||03:26 PM||||Come on guys lets get him!!!!! lol  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Carl (85 Fiero) My Website's  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/HMP" TARGET=_blank>Haze Mustang Performance</A>   &  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/WS6_Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero</A>||||reg||14
