Z||000018||shain||07-07-2000||03:45 AM||||well, it looks like i'll most likely buy one. its not that i wont have the money, i just want the car for crusin around in the summer!<BR>noone should ever subject a fiero to a harsh canadian winter! and its gonna suck if im under it on a hot sunny day rather than in it!!<BR>and please people, dont get so snappy when i say my car is a peice of crap! it just that as of late, my ass hurts from havin'to ride my bike everywhere!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR> <BR>i wont mind doing minor,(even somewhat complicated) repairs. ive got plenty of experience fixing all kinds of cars and i might be able to show you guys a thing or two! you think just cause im 19 im a rookie! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>im just sick of people turning around and expecting to see a garbage truck every time i cruise down the road!! cause thats what it sounds like.<P>p.s- any thing you can tell me aboot the 4 spd trans v.s. the 5 spd?? good?? bad?? <P>thanx!<BR>--------------<BR>shain<BR>85 2m4<BR>cummings turbo deisel<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000019||2M4 Dale||07-08-2000||09:51 AM||||I believe I may have the best runnin' Duck around. No problems ... GREAT fuel economy ... and it's kinda quick. (no V6 BUT) Get that duck runnin' right and you'll love it too !<P>By the way , I have a 85 Duck engine for sale. Seems to run pretty good.||||reg||10
Z||000020||lowCG||07-08-2000||10:13 AM||||I'd start with the ignition,make sure all is good there,then try playing with the TPS,and IAC a little bit at a time,it'll run a lot better if the compression is alright and you take a little time to fuss with it a bit.<BR>Then right after you get it running better,do something cosmetic to make you feel better about the car,and  in a couple years,the perfect GT will come along.<BR>Or do what I did while in school;sell the 4 cylinder,take a bus,buy the V6 car a couple years later.Not sure if I would do the FWD thing again however,that just seems like too much deprivation of character for one person to have to suffer.||||reg||1
Z||000021||ChadMan||07-08-2000||11:33 AM||||You'll never confuse the Iron Duke's sound for a Porsche 944 or a Fiat Spyder, that's for sure.  <P>But you can make it sound better (Don't laugh) - if your valve cover bolts are loose you'll hear a LOT more clatter from the valvetrain.  I replaced my valve cover gasket because it was leaking and was amazed by how much quieter the valve clatter seemed.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Vader||07-08-2000||11:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChadMan:<BR><B>You'll never confuse the Iron Duke's sound for a Porsche 944 or a Fiat Spyder, that's for sure.  <P>But you can make it sound better ...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It sounds even better with a fairly open exhaust on the 355SBC stuffed between the strut towers...Then there's no mistaking it for a P.O.S. 944.<P>Later,<BR>Vader<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000023||88formula||07-08-2000||12:42 PM||||shain, I feel for you man. My first fiero was an 87 coupe and though it was fun to drive it was a slow automatic gas guzzling piece of junk that sound like a diesel engine when it was idling. I now have an 88 formula that I bought with 48000 miles on it. The owner had garaged it and had all the service records for this car showing that it had been taken care of. Even though it was a low mileage well taken care of car it still had some problems. The exhaust manifold cracked for the second time in the front (the first time it did was with the original owner) and the brakes are the original brakes so it needs a brake job (expensive for 88). I am glade though that I have this formula. It rides better than that couple ever could, it handles way better, and being a five speed it is just plain fun. <P>So if you don’t like your couple than don’t keep it. Find as new a model fiero with the v6 as you can and buy. Then sell your couple for as much as you can get for it.<P>Keep in mind though that the newest fiero you can buy is twelve years old and no matter how well it was maintained it will have problems just like any other twelve year old car will. This means you will spend some of your summer under your car. But on the flip side wouldn’t you rather be under a v6 model than an Iron Duke? <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 07-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||86SE||07-08-2000||12:58 PM||||I would say you NEED a V6 Fiero.  You will be very surprized how much more power they have!  If you are looking for the most power get an 85-86 Fiero GT or 86 SE.  The 85-86 V6 were all high-output which means 5 more horsepower and 5 more foot lbs of torque.  Not much, but with other small cheap engine mods the power will stack up.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Fiero||||reg||11
Z||000025||shain||07-10-2000||04:53 AM||||you dont know wht your talking aboot 86se. the horse power rating for ALL fieros (v6) is 140 @5100 rpm. in  1985 the "new" v6 was actualy just the 2.8 seen in early gm x- cars. the only difference is that it had HO cylinder heads and camshaft. that doesnt meake it a HO!  why would GM only put the "HO" in the 85 and 86 cars if it was trying to make the car better and more sales??somthin smells fishy here!!<P><BR>anyways....  can any one tell me their opinions on the 4 spd v.s the 5 spd tranny's??? pplleeeaassee!! ||||reg||1
